    subjectLabel: 'Biology', topicLabel: 'Reproduction and Growth', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'In a flower the male part is the stamen, made of two pieces: the anther (the bag where pollen is made) sitting on top of the filament (the thin stalk that holds it up). The female part is the carpel, whose tip is the stigma (the surface that catches pollen). You can tell a wind-pollinated flower from an insect-pollinated one by its adaptations: the anthers hang outside the flower on long filaments so the wind shakes pollen out, the stigmas are large and feathery to catch drifting pollen from the air, the pollen is small and light, and there are no big bright petals, scent or nectar because no animal needs attracting. Pollination is the transfer of pollen from anther to stigma; fertilisation happens afterwards inside the ovule.',
