    gist: 'A thematic (choropleth-style) map shows how a single variable varies across space - here, the average hours of bright sunshine each county receives per year, a direct measure of how solar energy is distributed across Ireland. The east and south-east (Wexford, Waterford) record the most sunshine because they are sheltered in the rain-shadow of the prevailing south-westerly winds, while the cloudier, wetter Atlantic west and north-west (Mayo, Donegal) record the least. The contour lines that would join places of equal sunshine on such a map are called isohels. To answer map questions, read the value pills carefully, compare them to spot the highest and lowest, and subtract the two stated values for a difference - the arithmetic is where marks are most often lost.',
