    focus: 'Heart cycle (diagram)',
    gist: 'The heart cycle (cardiac cycle) has two phases: systole, when cardiac muscle CONTRACTS to push blood out, and diastole, when it relaxes and the chambers refill. The trick to reading a heart-cycle diagram is to follow the arrows and watch the valves. When the ATRIA contract, blood is pushed down into the ventricles. When the VENTRICLES contract (ventricular systole), the atrioventricular (AV) valves snap shut so blood can\'t flow back, the semilunar valves open, and blood is forced UP and OUT into the arteries. So a diagram with arrows pointing up out of the ventricles into the arteries, with the AV valves closed, is showing ventricular contraction. The lubb-dupp sounds of each heartbeat are simply the valves closing.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Read the arrows and the valves, not the shape',
      text: 'Don\'t guess from how the heart is drawn. Ventricles contracting = blood being forced OUT, so look for arrows pointing up and out into the arteries, AV valves shut and semilunar valves open. Atria contracting = arrows pushing down into the ventricles. Naming the diagram is only half the marks — the examiner wants the justification (which valves are open/closed, or which way the blood is flowing), so always state the valve evidence.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'In the heart-cycle diagram above, the three hearts X, Y and Z show different stages. Which diagram shows the ventricles contracting, and how can you tell from the diagram?',
      modelAnswer: 'Diagram Z shows the ventricles contracting (ventricular systole). You can tell because the arrows point up and out of the ventricles into the arteries — blood is being forced out of the heart. This happens because ventricular contraction reduces the ventricle volume, snapping the atrioventricular (AV) valves shut so blood cannot flow back into the atria, while the semilunar valves are pushed open to let blood leave through the arteries.',
      needed: ['Diagram Z is the one with the ventricles contracting', 'Justification from the diagram: arrows show blood flowing up/out of the ventricles into the arteries (out of the heart)', 'Valve evidence: semilunar valves open AND AV valves closed (or ventricle volume reduced)'],
    },
    source: 'SEC Leaving Certificate Biology 2022 Higher Level marking scheme, Q17(d) — ventricles contracting = Z; justification: ventricle volumes reduced OR semilunar valves open OR AV valves closed OR blood flowing into arteries (out of heart); contracting = systole, not contracting = diastole; heart sounds caused by valves closing',
