    id: 'he-foodscience-sugar-properties',
    subjectId: 'home-economics', topicId: 'home-economics-0-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Home Economics', topicLabel: 'Food Science & Food Commodities',
    focus: 'Properties of sugar in cooking', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Sugar behaves predictably when you cook with it, and each behaviour has a culinary use. Solubility: sugar is a white crystalline solid that dissolves in water (faster when heated) to form a syrup. Sweetness: relative sweetness varies (sucrose 100, glucose 75, fructose 170, lactose 15). Aeration: sugar denatures egg protein so whisked egg-and-sugar traps air (sponges, meringues). Caramelisation: heated sugar melts and browns into caramel between 104C and 177C. Maillard reaction: a non-enzymic browning between sugars and amino acids in dry heat. Crystallisation: too much sugar in a liquid forms crystals as it cools.',
