      modelAnswer: 'Solubility: sugar is a white crystalline compound that dissolves in water, and solubility increases when the water is heated, forming a syrup — used to make icing or to bottle/can fruit. Aeration: sugar denatures egg protein so that whisking egg with sugar traps air and the mixture becomes aerated — used to make a sponge cake or meringues. Caramelisation: when sugar is heated it melts and turns to a brown caramel, passing through gradual stages between 104C and 177C before it eventually carbonises — used to make creme caramel or creme brulee. (The Maillard reaction — non-enzymic browning between sugar and amino acids in dry heat, as on roast potatoes — would also be accepted as a third property.)',
