    id: 'he-homedesign-planning-professionals',
    subjectId: 'home-economics', topicId: 'home-economics-3-0',
    subjectLabel: 'Home Economics', topicLabel: 'Home Design & Management',
    focus: 'Planning permission & the professionals who build a house', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Before any building work starts on a site you must get FULL PLANNING PERMISSION from the local planning authority. The procedure: submit a detailed application to the local planning authority including site maps showing the proposed location, house plans and interior plans, and details of the materials to be used; pay the planning fee; publish a planning notice in a local newspaper; and put a site notice up at the proposed development. The authority then decides — it may grant permission, request further information, or refuse. Three professionals help design and build the house. The ARCHITECT advises on the choice of site, designs and draws up the house plans, makes the planning application and may oversee construction. The ENGINEER advises on the structure of the house, oversees the project and advises on building problems. The SOLICITOR deals with the legal side — building and planning regulations, making sure the title deeds are in order, checking rights of way, and is needed if you are applying for a mortgage.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Three professionals = three separate 5-mark blocks: give a distinct job for EACH',
      text: 'The 2024 OL scheme (Q1(b)(ii)) marks architect, engineer and solicitor as three independent blocks (1 point @ 5 marks each, graded 5:3:0), so a vague "they all help build the house" earns nothing. Tie one specific role to each: architect = draws up the house plans and makes the planning application; engineer = advises on the structure of the house; solicitor = handles the legal side (title deeds, planning regulations, the mortgage). The planning-permission procedure (Q1(b)(i)) is its own separate 15-mark block (3 points @ 5 marks) — name three concrete steps (apply to the local planning authority with site maps and plans, newspaper notice, site notice), not just "you need permission". The two parts together are worth 30 marks.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Describe the procedure to follow when applying for full planning permission to build a house, and discuss the role of the following professional services when designing and building it: architect, engineer and solicitor.',
      modelAnswer: 'Planning permission must be obtained from the local planning authority before any building work starts. You submit a detailed application to the local planning authority that includes site maps showing the proposed location of the building, the house plans and interior plans, and details of the materials to be used, together with the planning fee; you publish a planning notice in a local newspaper and place a notice at the site of the proposed development. The authority then grants permission, requests further information, or refuses. Architect: advises on the choice of site, designs and draws up the house plans, makes the planning application and may oversee the construction. Engineer: provides advice on the structure of the house, oversees the project and advises on any building problems. Solicitor: deals with the legal aspects — advises on building and planning regulations, ensures the title deeds are in order, checks rights of way, and must be engaged if you are applying for a mortgage.',
      needed: ['Planning procedure: apply to the local planning authority before building, submitting site maps, house/interior plans and details of materials, plus the planning fee, with a newspaper notice and a site notice', 'Architect = designs and draws up the house plans / makes the planning application (and may oversee construction)', 'Engineer = advises on the structure of the house / oversees the project', 'Solicitor = handles the legal side (title deeds, planning regulations, mortgage)'],
    },
    source: 'LC Home Economics — Section C, Elective 1: Home Design & Management; Marking scheme 2024 OL Q1(b)(i) (planning-permission procedure, 3 points @ 5 marks = 15) and Q1(b)(ii) (architect/engineer/solicitor, 1 point @ 5 marks each = 15) — 30 marks in total',
    marksWeight: 30,
