    gist: 'Stakeholders in business deal with each other in two ways. A co-operative relationship is where both parties work towards a common goal, so both gain — a WIN-WIN situation (e.g. an employer funds training and the upskilled employee becomes more productive). A competitive relationship is where parties pursue a mutually exclusive goal, so one gains at the other\'s expense — a WIN-LOSE situation (e.g. the employee wants a pay rise while the employer wants to cut pay to protect profit). The same two parties (employer/employee, investor/manager, two suppliers) can be co-operative on one issue and competitive on another.',
