    id: 'biz-ratios-gearing',
    subjectId: 'business', topicId: 'business-3-14',
    subjectLabel: 'Business', topicLabel: 'Profitability and Liquidity Ratios',
    focus: 'Gearing & Debt/Equity Ratio', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Gearing measures financial risk: how much of a company\'s funding comes from DEBT (long-term loans/debentures) versus EQUITY (issued/ordinary share capital + reserves/retained earnings). The Debt/Equity ratio = Debt ÷ Equity, written as a ratio out of 1 (e.g. 0.5:1). A company is HIGHLY geared when debt makes up MORE than half of total capital employed — i.e. the ratio is above 1:1, where debt exceeds equity. It is LOWLY geared when debt is below 50% of capital (ratio below 1:1). Highly geared = riskier, because loan interest must be paid even in a bad year, whereas dividends to shareholders are optional. At 0.5:1 a company is lowly geared.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Put only LOANS on the debt side, only share capital + reserves on the equity side',
      text: 'The classic slip is mixing the two sides up or adding the wrong figures. Debt = long-term loans/debentures ONLY. Equity = issued share capital + reserves/retained earnings (NOT authorised share capital — that\'s just the legal maximum it could issue, never used in the calculation). The 2025 HL scheme awards the workings 5 marks at 1 mark each, so set out Debt ÷ Equity, sub in the right figures, and finish as a ratio out of 1.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: '(a) Explain the term Gearing. (b) Korn Ltd\'s 2024 figures are: Authorised Share Capital €900,000; Issued Share Capital €550,000; Long-Term Loan €300,000; Reserves/Retained Earnings €50,000. Calculate the Debt/Equity Ratio and show your workings.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) Gearing is the amount of debt (long-term loans) a company uses to fund its operations in proportion to its equity (share capital + retained earnings); it is an indicator of financial risk. (b) Debt/Equity = Debt ÷ Equity = 300,000 ÷ (550,000 + 50,000) = 300,000 ÷ 600,000 = 0.5:1. (Authorised share capital of €900,000 is ignored.) At 0.5:1 the company is lowly geared.',
      needed: ['Gearing = proportion of debt to equity / financial risk indicator', 'Debt = long-term loan €300,000 only', 'Equity = issued share capital €550,000 + reserves €50,000 = €600,000 (NOT authorised capital)', 'Answer 0.5:1, shown as Debt ÷ Equity'],
    },
    source: 'LC Business — Unit 4: Managing 2 (Profitability and Liquidity Ratios). SEC Leaving Certificate Business 2025 Higher Level, Q7. Marking scheme 2025: \'Explain the term Gearing\' (3+2); \'Calculate the Debt/Equity Ratio. Show your workings\' 5@1m, answer 0.5:1 (300,000 ÷ (550,000 + 50,000)).',
    marksWeight: 10,
