    gist: 'Both are methods of business expansion, but the ownership differs completely. A strategic alliance is when two or more independent firms agree to co-operate and share resources and expertise for the mutual benefit of all parties — but each firm stays completely independent in law and keeps its own separate trading identity. A merger is a friendly, voluntary amalgamation of two or more businesses, where they combine to form a single new legal entity (e.g. Glanbia plc was formed in 1997 from the merger of Avonmore Foods plc and Waterford Foods plc). So: an alliance = co-operation while staying separate; a merger = becoming one new company.',
