    id: 'fr-grammaire-pronoms-et-futur',
    subjectId: 'french', topicId: 'french-1-1',
    subjectLabel: 'French', topicLabel: 'Exploring Meaning',
    focus: 'Les pronoms + le futur (aller + infinitif)', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'PRONOUNS let you avoid repeating a noun. Subject pronouns: je, tu, il/elle, nous, vous, ils/elles. After a preposition (with, for…) you need a STRESSED pronoun: \'avec moi\', \'pour lui\', \'avec elle\' — never \'avec je\'. Object pronouns go BEFORE the verb: \'je le vois\' (I see it/him), \'je la connais\' (I know her), \'je les aime\' (I like them). For the FUTURE, the easy reliable way is the near future = aller (present) + infinitive: \'je VAIS visiter\' (I\'m going to visit), \'nous ALLONS partir\' (we\'re going to leave), \'ils VONT manger\'. The true future tense adds endings to the infinitive: \'je visiterai\', \'nous ferons\' (we will do), \'je serai\' (I will be).',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Pronoun position is heard — \'il a vu elle\' loses the mark',
      text: 'Object pronouns sit IN FRONT of the verb in French. The HL scheme\'s own example penalises \'il a serré elle\' — the correct French is \'il l\'a serrée\' (the pronoun goes before). And after prepositions you must switch to the stressed form: \'pour moi\', not \'pour je\'. For the future, if the full future tense feels shaky, fall back on \'aller + infinitif\' — it\'s correct, it\'s heard as correct, and it keeps you in the higher Language band (\'tenses correct more often than incorrect\').',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Récrivez avec un pronom objet, puis mettez la dernière phrase au futur proche : « Je regarde le film. » → ? « Demain, je (visiter) Paris avec mon frère. » (Rewrite the first sentence using an object pronoun; put the second into the near future.)',
      modelAnswer: '« Je LE regarde. » (le film → masculine object pronoun \'le\', placed BEFORE the verb: \'je le regarde\' = I watch it.) « Demain, je VAIS VISITER Paris avec mon frère. » (near future = aller in the present (je vais) + the infinitive \'visiter\' = tomorrow I\'m going to visit Paris with my brother. Note \'avec mon frère\' — after \'avec\' you keep the noun, but if it were a pronoun it would be the stressed \'avec lui\'.)',
      needed: ['le film → \'le\', and the object pronoun goes BEFORE the verb (\'je le regarde\')', 'Near future = aller (vais/vas/va/allons/allez/vont) + infinitive (\'je vais visiter\')', 'After a preposition use a stressed pronoun (avec lui / pour moi), not \'avec il / pour je\''],
    },
    source: 'Grammaire — Les pronoms et le futur. Grounded in LC French Higher Level marking scheme 2024, Appendix 1 grammar-waiver note (\'il a serré elle\' = incorrect pronoun, penalised; \'il l\'a serré\' agreement waived); LC French Ordinary Level marking scheme 2024 \'Filling Gaps\' answer key and passage (\'allons\', \'ferons\'; near future \'Nous allons visiter\').',
