    id: 'chem-molecule-shapes-vsepr',
    subjectId: 'chemistry', topicId: 'chemistry-1-4',
    subjectLabel: 'Chemistry', topicLabel: 'Molecule Shapes and Forces',
    focus: 'VSEPR molecule shapes', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Electron-pair repulsion theory (VSEPR) says the pairs of electrons around a central atom push as far apart as possible, and that fixes the molecule\'s shape. Count the electron pairs (bonds + lone pairs) on the central atom. CH4 has 4 bond pairs and no lone pairs, so it is a perfect tetrahedron (109.5 degrees). BF3 has 3 bond pairs, so it is trigonal planar (120 degrees). NH3 also has 4 pairs, but one is a lone pair, so its three bonds are squeezed into a pyramidal shape with a smaller 107 degrees angle. A lone pair repels harder than a bond pair, so it closes the bond angle.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'A lone pair squeezes the bond angle — that\'s the mark',
      text: 'NH3 isn\'t flat like BF3 because the lone pair on nitrogen repels more strongly than a bond pair (lone-pair/bond-pair repulsion is stronger than bond-pair/bond-pair). That extra push tips the molecule into a pyramid and shrinks the angle from 109.5 to 107 degrees. Naming the lone pair as the reason is what earns the marks — \'it just is pyramidal\' does not.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Look at the table. CH4 has a bond angle of 109.5 degrees but NH3 (which also has four electron pairs around its central atom) has only 107.0 degrees. (i) Name the shape of NH3. (ii) Explain, using electron-pair repulsion, why its bond angle is smaller than that of CH4.',
      modelAnswer: '(i) Pyramidal (trigonal pyramidal). (ii) Both have four electron pairs around the central atom, but in NH3 one of those pairs is a lone pair. A lone pair repels the bonding pairs more strongly than a bonding pair does (lone-pair/bond-pair repulsion is greater than bond-pair/bond-pair). This extra repulsion pushes the three N-H bonds closer together, reducing the angle from 109.5 to 107 degrees.',
      needed: ['Shape of NH3 is pyramidal', 'NH3 has a lone pair (4 pairs, one a lone pair)', 'Lone-pair repulsion is stronger than bond-pair repulsion', 'So the bonds are pushed closer / angle reduced'],
    },
    source: 'LC Chemistry — 2.5 Molecule Shapes and Forces; grounded in SEC 2021 Higher Level Q10(a) and its marking scheme (NH3 not trigonal planar because a lone pair is present / 4 electron pairs; bond angle 107 < 109.5). The lone-pair/bond-pair repulsion ordering is standard LC VSEPR theory used to explain why the angle is reduced.',
