    focus: 'Electronegativity trends', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Electronegativity is the relative attraction an atom (its nucleus) has for the shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond. The graph shows two trends. Across Period 2 (Li to F) it rises: the effective nuclear charge increases while the atomic radius shrinks, so the nucleus pulls the bonding electrons harder. Down Group 17 (F to At) it falls: extra shells make the atom bigger and add more inner-electron shielding, so the bonding pair is further from the nucleus and held less tightly. The most electronegative element is fluorine, top-right.',
