    gist: 'In a titration you find an unknown concentration by reacting it with a solution of KNOWN concentration. Worked example (SEC 2023 OL): 25.0 cm3 portions of a 0.05 M sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) standard solution are titrated with hydrochloric acid (HCl) of unknown concentration; equation 2HCl + Na2CO3 -> 2NaCl + H2O + CO2. The pipette delivers exactly 25.0 cm3 of Na2CO3 into the conical flask; the burette adds the HCl. Titres: rough 23.1, first accurate 22.5, second accurate 22.4 cm3. Step 1 average = use only the two ACCURATE titres: (22.5 + 22.4)/2 = 22.45 cm3 (the rough one is discarded). Step 2 use the formula (Ma x Va)/na = (Mb x Vb)/nb with HCl as a (na = 2) and Na2CO3 as b (nb = 1): Ma x 22.45 / 2 = 0.05 x 25.0 / 1, so Ma = (0.05 x 25.0 x 2)/22.45 = 2.5/22.45 = 0.111 mol/L HCl.',
