    id: 'chem-gas-laws-2022-ol-charles-law',
    subjectId: 'chemistry', topicId: 'chemistry-2-1',
    subjectLabel: 'Chemistry', topicLabel: '3.2 Gas Laws',
    focus: 'Charles\' law calculation (volume and temperature)', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Charles\' law: at constant pressure, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is DIRECTLY PROPORTIONAL to its temperature on the KELVIN scale - so V1/T1 = V2/T2. The non-negotiable step is converting every Celsius temperature to kelvin by adding 273 BEFORE you put it into the formula. Worked example (SEC 2022 OL): a gas sample is 1.5 litres at 20 C; find its volume at 750 C at the same pressure. Step 1 convert: T1 = 20 + 273 = 293 K, T2 = 750 + 273 = 1023 K. Step 2 V2 = V1 x T2/T1 = 1.5 x 1023/293 = 5.2 litres. (Boyle\'s law is the partner rule: at constant temperature, volume is inversely proportional to pressure, P1V1 = P2V2.)',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Convert to KELVIN first - the scheme marks it as \'Kelvin essential\'',
      text: 'The single biggest mark-loss is plugging Celsius straight into V1/T1 = V2/T2. The marking scheme literally writes \'Kelvin essential\': you must add 273 to each temperature first (20 C -> 293 K, 750 C -> 1023 K). Using 20 and 750 instead gives a nonsense answer (about 56 litres). Once in kelvin, the gas got much hotter, so the volume must get BIGGER - a quick sanity check that your fraction (1023/293) is the right way up.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'A gas sample has a volume of 1.5 litres at 20 C. Calculate its volume at 750 C at the same (constant) pressure, correct to one decimal place, and name the law used.',
      modelAnswer: 'Convert to kelvin: T1 = 20 + 273 = 293 K, T2 = 750 + 273 = 1023 K. Charles\' law: V1/T1 = V2/T2, so 1.5/293 = V2/1023, giving V2 = 1.5 x 1023/293 = 5.2 litres. The law used is Charles\' law (the combined gas law also accepted).',
      needed: ['Convert both temperatures to kelvin: 293 K and 1023 K (add 273)', 'Apply V1/T1 = V2/T2 : 1.5/293 = V2/1023', 'V2 = 5.2 litres', 'Name the law: Charles\' law'],
    },
    source: '3.2 Gas Laws - grounded in SEC Leaving Certificate Chemistry 2022 Ordinary Level, Q11(b)(iv) and its marking scheme (1.5/293 = V2/1023, \'Kelvin essential\', V2 = 5.2 litres, Charles\' law / combined gas law); Charles\' law \'directly proportional\' also examined 2021 OL Q4(e) and 2025 OL Q4(d)',
    marksWeight: 9,
