    focus: 'Ethene: planar carbon + addition reactions', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Ethene is C2H4, the simplest alkene. Each carbon is joined to the other by a C=C double bond and to two H atoms, so the geometry around each carbon is PLANAR (flat), not tetrahedral - that double bond is the whole story. Because the C=C bond is reactive, ethene undergoes ADDITION reactions: a small molecule adds across the double bond and the product has only single bonds. Add H2 (with a catalyst) to get ethane; add HCl to get chloroethane (C2H5Cl); add Cl2 to get 1,2-dichloroethane (C2H4Cl2). Heat ethanol vapour over Al2O3 and you run it the other way (elimination/dehydration) to make ethene; polymerise many ethene molecules to get polyethene.',
