    focus: 'Saturated alkanes, tetrahedral carbon and structural isomers', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'An alkane is a SATURATED hydrocarbon: it contains only single carbon-carbon bonds (no double or triple bonds), so each carbon is bonded to four other atoms and its geometry is TETRAHEDRAL. \'Saturated\' means it holds the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms and does not undergo addition reactions. Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but a different arrangement of atoms. C5H12 has exactly three structural isomers: pentane (straight chain), 2-methylbutane (one branch) and 2,2-dimethylpropane (two methyl branches on the central carbon). Branching changes the boiling point and the octane number even though the formula is identical.',
