    focus: 'Domestic electricity: choosing the right fuse', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Domestic electricity is a frequent OL passage question. Two ideas earn the marks. First, how a fuse works: it is a thin wire that melts and breaks the circuit if too much current flows. Second, choosing a fuse for an appliance: find the appliance\'s operating current with I = P/V (mains V = 230 V), then pick the SMALLEST listed fuse that is still ABOVE that current. Students slip by picking a fuse below the working current (it would blow in normal use) or forgetting that P = VI gives the current.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'I = P/V, then the next fuse UP',
      text: 'Do not guess the fuse — calculate the current first. Rearrange P = VI to I = P/V using the 230 V mains supply, then choose the lowest available fuse rating that is still larger than that current. For a 2300 W kettle, I = 2300/230 = 10 A, so a 3 A or 5 A fuse would blow during normal use; the 13 A fuse is the correct choice. When explaining how a fuse works, the marking word is \'melts\' (or breaks) when the current is too high.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'An electric kettle has a power rating of 2300 W and runs from the 230 V mains supply. (a) Explain how a fuse works. (b) From the list 3 A, 5 A, 13 A, identify the most appropriate fuse for the kettle, showing your reasoning.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) A fuse contains a thin wire that melts (and breaks the circuit) if the current becomes too high, cutting off the supply and protecting the appliance and wiring. (b) Operating current: I = P/V = 2300/230 = 10 A. The fuse must be rated just above the normal current, so 3 A and 5 A would melt during normal use. The most appropriate fuse is 13 A.',
      needed: ['A fuse melts / breaks the circuit if the current is too high', 'I = P/V = 2300/230 = 10 A', '13 A fuse chosen (next rating above the 10 A operating current)'],
    },
    source: 'Domestic circuits and safety — SEC LC Physics 2025 Ordinary, Q13(iii)+(iv) + marking scheme (fuse \'melts / breaks if current is too high\'; 13 A)',
    marksWeight: 14,
