    id: 'phys-photoelectric-max-speed-2022-hl',
    subjectId: 'physics', topicId: 'physics-7-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Physics', topicLabel: 'Photoelectric Emission',
    focus: 'Photoelectric: max electron speed from hf = phi + KE', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'The photoelectric question gives you light (a wavelength or frequency) hitting a metal with a known work function, and asks for the maximum speed of the emitted electrons. The whole thing is one equation: hf = phi + 1/2 m v^2. Students lose marks by mixing units - the work function is given in eV but everything in the equation must be in joules. Convert wavelength to frequency, convert eV to J, subtract to get the kinetic energy, then solve for v.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Convert eV to joules and wavelength to frequency first',
      text: 'Before touching hf = phi + 1/2 m v^2, do two conversions: f = c / lambda (so 450 nm becomes 6.67x10^14 Hz), and phi in joules = (eV value) x 1.6x10^-19 (so 2.4 eV becomes 3.84x10^-19 J). Then KE = hf - phi = 4.42x10^-19 - 3.84x10^-19 = 5.8x10^-20 J, and v = sqrt(2 x KE / m_e). The scheme awards the formula and each conversion separately, so even a slip in the final arithmetic still banks the conversion marks - never leave eV unconverted, that is the single most common mark-loss here.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Light of wavelength 450 nm is incident on a metal that has a work function of 2.4 eV. Calculate the maximum speed of the emitted electrons. (Take c = 3.0x10^8 m s^-1, h = 6.63x10^-34 J s, mass of electron = 9.11x10^-31 kg, 1 eV = 1.6x10^-19 J.)',
      modelAnswer: 'Use Einstein\'s photoelectric law hf = phi + 1/2 m v^2.\nFrequency: f = c / lambda = (3.0x10^8) / (450x10^-9) = 6.67x10^14 Hz.\nEnergy of photon: hf = (6.63x10^-34)(6.67x10^14) = 4.42x10^-19 J.\nWork function in joules: phi = (2.4)(1.6x10^-19) = 3.84x10^-19 J.\nMaximum kinetic energy: 1/2 m v^2 = hf - phi = 4.42x10^-19 - 3.84x10^-19 = 5.8x10^-20 J.\nSpeed: v = sqrt(2 x 5.8x10^-20 / 9.11x10^-31) = sqrt(1.27x10^11) = 3.56x10^5 m s^-1.',
      needed: ['f = c/lambda gives f = 6.67x10^14 Hz (convert wavelength to frequency)', 'phi = (2.4)(1.6x10^-19) = 3.84x10^-19 J (convert eV to joules)', 'v = 3.56x10^5 m s^-1 (within rounding)'],
    },
    source: 'Photoelectric Emission - SEC LC Physics 2022 Higher, Q14(c)(ii) + marking scheme (f = 6.67x10^14 Hz; phi = 3.84x10^-19 J; v = 3.56x10^5 m s^-1)',
