    id: 'phys-sound-fundamental-freq-hl-lyre-string-2022',
    subjectId: 'physics', topicId: 'physics-4-3',
    subjectLabel: 'Physics', topicLabel: 'Vibrations in Strings',
    focus: 'Fundamental frequency of a stretched string', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'The fundamental frequency of a stretched string is f = (1/2L)*sqrt(T/mu), where L is the vibrating length, T is the tension and mu is the mass per unit length. The killer detail: mu = total mass / total length of the string, but L is only the distance between the two points the string is fixed/stretched across. These two lengths are often different, and using the wrong one for either quantity is the classic mark-loss. Rearrange for the unknown before substituting, and remember f is proportional to sqrt(T), so changing the tension by a factor scales f by the square root of that factor.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Separate the two lengths: mu uses the WHOLE string, L is only the span between the fixings',
      text: 'Compute mu from the full string: mu = m/l_full = 0.00021/0.65 = 3.23e-4 kg m^-1. Then use the VIBRATING length L = 0.341 m (the distance between the two points) in f = (1/2L)*sqrt(T/mu). Rearrange: T = mu*(2*L*f)^2 = 3.23e-4*(2*0.341*440)^2 = 29.1 N. Students who put 0.65 m in for L get a wrong tension; the string\'s full length only ever feeds mu. For part (vi), since f is proportional to sqrt(T), quartering T halves f: 440 -> 220 Hz.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'A 65 cm string of mass 0.21 g is stretched between two points of a lyre which are 34.1 cm apart. It is required to vibrate at a fundamental frequency of 440 Hz. (i) Calculate the tension that must be applied to the string. (ii) Determine the new fundamental frequency if the tension is then reduced by a factor of four.',
      modelAnswer: '(i) Mass per unit length uses the full string: mu = m/l = (0.21e-3 kg)/(0.65 m) = 3.23e-4 kg m^-1. The fundamental frequency is f = (1/2L)*sqrt(T/mu), where the vibrating length L = 0.341 m (the gap between the two fixing points), not 0.65 m. Rearranging: T = mu*(2*L*f)^2 = 3.23e-4*(2 x 0.341 x 440)^2 = 3.23e-4 x (300.08)^2 = 29.1 N. (ii) f is proportional to sqrt(T). Reducing T by a factor of four reduces f by a factor of sqrt(4) = 2, so the new frequency = 440/2 = 220 Hz.',
      needed: ['mu = m/l = 0.00021/0.65 = 3.23e-4 kg m^-1 (mass per unit length from the FULL string length)', 'Use f = (1/2L)*sqrt(T/mu) with vibrating length L = 0.341 m and rearrange to T = mu*(2Lf)^2 = 29.1 N', 'f proportional to sqrt(T): quartering T halves f, giving 220 Hz'],
    },
    source: 'Vibrations in Strings (fundamental frequency of a stretched string) — SEC LC Physics 2022 Higher Level, Q11 parts (v) and (vi) + marking scheme (mu = 0.00021/0.65 = 0.000323 kg m^-1; T = 29.1 N; reduced-tension frequency = 220 Hz)',
    marksWeight: 16,
