      modelAnswer: 'Impact 1 - a climate of terror and the Great Purge: the show trials of 1936-1938 publicly condemned Old Bolsheviks such as Zinoviev, Kamenev and later Bukharin, and they signalled and fuelled the wider Great Terror in which hundreds of thousands were arrested or executed by the NKVD. This spread fear through Soviet society, deterring any opposition to Stalin. Impact 2 - the weakening of the Red Army: the trials extended into a purge of the military, including the execution of leading commanders such as Marshal Tukhachevsky in 1937. The loss of experienced officers gravely damaged the Red Army\'s leadership and is widely seen as contributing to its early disasters when Germany invaded in 1941. Each paragraph is a separate, relevant impact with a supporting fact, so each builds its own Cumulative Mark rather than repeating one idea.',
