      modelAnswer: '(i) The marks come from Cumulative Mark (max 60) for statements that are relevant to the question PLUS an Overall Evaluation (max 40) that rewards answering the question rather than mere narrative. Accurate narration of the risings is not the same as saying what they CONTRIBUTED, so most of it is off-question and earns few significant relevant statements. Because the question names two elements (Young Ireland AND Fenianism) and the student really only handles Fenianism, the scheme also caps the Cumulative Mark at 50 instead of 60. (ii) Model SRS: \'Young Ireland contributed a romantic, cultural nationalism: through The Nation newspaper and the writings of Thomas Davis it spread the idea of a shared Irish identity above religious division, an idea later movements drew on.\' Then: \'Fenianism contributed the tradition of physical-force separatism and a disciplined oath-bound organisation (the IRB); though the 1867 rising failed, the movement and events like the Manchester executions kept the demand for a republic alive into the 20th century.\' Each sentence is one distinct point that directly answers \'contributed\', and the two together cover both named elements.',
