      modelAnswer: '(i) A positive externality is a benefit enjoyed by a third party. Example: when people are vaccinated, OTHERS are protected from catching the virus - the spread of the virus is reduced, so even unvaccinated people benefit. (Other valid spillovers: reduced pressure on hospitals / emergency departments, so they operate more efficiently; society and businesses can reopen and function normally.) (ii) Opportunity cost = the best alternative forgone. The money the government spent on the vaccine programme could have been used to provide other services - e.g. childcare subsidies, respite for carers, or improved pensions. That forgone next-best use is the opportunity cost.',
