    id: 'econ-3-3-ol-cost-push-inflation',
    subjectId: 'economics', topicId: 'economics-3-3',
    subjectLabel: 'Economics', topicLabel: 'Monetary Policy & Prices',
    focus: 'Cost-push inflation + why the ECB worries', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Cost-push inflation has a specific cause: the COSTS of the factors of production rise (wages, raw materials, energy) without any matching gain in productivity, so firms pass those higher costs on to consumers as higher prices. Lots of OL marks are lost here because students just write \'inflation is when prices rise\' — that is a definition of inflation in general, not of cost-PUSH inflation, so it does not earn the term. You need to name the mechanism (rising input costs, no productivity gain, costs passed on). The same logic explains why the ECB worries: its whole job is price stability (inflation close to but below 2%), and high inflation erodes purchasing power and can trigger a wage-price spiral.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Name the cause, not just the symptom',
      text: 'When the question says \'cost-push\', do not just say \'prices go up\'. Pin it to the cause: the costs of the factors of production rise (e.g. more expensive raw materials, wages, energy) WITHOUT a matching rise in productivity, and firms then pass that cost on to consumers as higher prices. The toilet-paper example in the 2024 paper is exactly this — eco-friendly raw materials cost more, so the price rose 15.6%. Say \'rising input costs passed on to consumers\', and you have named the term the scheme is looking for, instead of describing inflation in general.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: '(i) Explain what is meant by the economic term cost-push inflation. (ii) The European Central Bank (ECB) is concerned about inflation rates in the euro area. Give one reason why the ECB is concerned about inflation.',
      modelAnswer: '(i) Cost-push inflation occurs when the source of upward pressure on prices is a rise in the costs of the factors of production (for example wages, raw materials or energy) without a corresponding improvement in productivity. Firms then pass this increase in costs on to consumers in the form of higher prices. It can also take the form of imported inflation, where the price of imported raw materials rises and pushes up the price of finished goods at home. (ii) The ECB has a price-stability mandate: its primary objective is to keep euro-area inflation close to, but below, 2% over the medium term. When inflation is well above this target, price stability is lost and consumers\' purchasing power is eroded, reducing their real income and standard of living and potentially slowing economic growth.',
      needed: ['Cost-push inflation: rising costs of the factors of production (cost rises without a corresponding improvement in productivity)', 'These increased costs are passed on to consumers in the form of higher prices', 'ECB concern: price-stability mandate / objective is inflation close to but below 2% over the medium term', 'High inflation erodes purchasing power / reduces real income and standard of living'],
    },
    source: 'Monetary Policy & Prices — SEC LC Economics 2024 Ordinary Level, Q13(c)(i) & (iii) + marking scheme (cost-push inflation 10 marks; ECB concern 4 marks per point; Q13(c) = 24 marks)',
