      modelAnswer: '(a) Flushing - the ewes are put on a rising/high plane of nutrition (good quality grass) in the weeks before mating to improve body condition and increase the ovulation rate, giving more lambs. (b) The raddle is a coloured marker on the ram\'s chest that marks the ewes he has served, so the farmer knows which ewes have been mated; the colour is changed every 13-15 days so any ewe that returns to heat (was not held) is marked in a new colour and identified. (c) The average ratio is 1:40 (one ram per 40 ewes).',
