    id: 'agsci-monohybrid-petal-colour-hl',
    subjectId: 'agricultural-science', topicId: 'agricultural-science-2-0',
    subjectLabel: 'Agricultural Science', topicLabel: 'Crops: Plant Physiology (genetic inheritance)',
    focus: 'Reading a cross ratio: the trait that DOMINATES the offspring is the dominant trait', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'When a cross gives roughly THREE of one type to ONE of the other in the offspring, that is the classic monohybrid signature and the MORE COMMON type is the DOMINANT trait. The 2025 paper crossed a purple-flowered potato with a white-flowered one and counted the offspring. Students lose the marks by guessing the dominant trait is the \'nicer\' colour, or by ignoring the numbers entirely. The rule is purely numerical: count the offspring, and whichever phenotype appears in the LARGER proportion (here close to 3:1) is dominant. Then link it to farming: knowing inheritance lets you select for beneficial traits like yield or disease resistance.',
