    id: 'agsci-liver-fluke-control-ol',
    subjectId: 'agricultural-science', topicId: 'agricultural-science-3-5',
    subjectLabel: 'Agricultural Science', topicLabel: 'Animals: Husbandry and Health (liver fluke)',
    focus: 'Controlling liver fluke: target the mud snail and the wet ground it needs', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Liver fluke thrives in Ireland\'s wet climate and costs farmers over €90 million a year. The exam wants you to connect the parasite to its environment: it needs WET, waterlogged ground because its secondary host, the mud snail, lives there. So every good control answer either dries out / fences off the wet habitat (removing the snail) or doses the animals with an anthelmintic. Students lose marks by giving only one type of control or by naming a treatment without explaining it targets the snail or the fluke.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Break the fluke\'s life cycle: drain/fence the wet ground (kills the mud snail host) AND dose with anthelmintic',
      text: 'Give controls that hit the life cycle, not just one trick. Fence off or drain wet areas to remove the mud snail (the fluke\'s secondary host); add lime to raise soil pH and discourage the snail; dose animals with an anthelmintic (flukicide); carry out faecal egg counts; operate a closed flock or breed from fluke-resistant breeds. State the environmental condition plainly: liver fluke needs WET / waterlogged / poorly-drained ground.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Liver fluke thrives in Ireland\'s wet climate and badly affects grazing sheep. (i) Outline ONE environmental condition that allows liver fluke to thrive. (ii) Suggest THREE ways a farmer can control or prevent liver fluke on the farm.',
      modelAnswer: '(i) Liver fluke thrives in wet, waterlogged or poorly-drained ground (excess water), because its secondary host, the mud snail, needs these damp conditions. (ii) Control/prevention: (1) fence off or drain wet areas to remove the mud snail habitat; (2) dose the animals with an anthelmintic (flukicide) and carry out faecal egg counts to time dosing; (3) add lime to the soil to raise the pH and discourage the mud snail, and/or operate a closed flock or breed from fluke-resistant breeds to build natural resistance.',
      needed: ['Environmental condition: wet / waterlogged / poorly-drained ground (excess water)', 'Control: fence off or drain wet areas (removes the mud snail, the fluke\'s secondary host)', 'Control: dose animals with an anthelmintic/flukicide (and/or faecal egg counts)', 'Control: add lime to raise soil pH OR closed flock / breed fluke-resistant animals'],
    },
    source: 'Animals: Husbandry and Health (liver fluke) — SEC LC Agricultural Science 2021 Ordinary Level, Q13(a)(ii),(iii) + marking scheme (environmental condition = waterlogged or wet poorly-drained areas; control = fence off wet areas to remove mud snail secondary host / add lime to increase pH / drain land / dose animals with anthelmintic / faecal egg counts / closed flock / breed fluke-resistant breeds)',
    marksWeight: 9,
