    id: 'agsci-crops-mgmt-strip-grazing-system-ol',
    subjectId: 'agricultural-science', topicId: 'agricultural-science-2-4',
    subjectLabel: 'Agricultural Science', topicLabel: 'Production: Management',
    focus: 'Grassland: strip grazing', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Grassland management questions reward precise grazing vocabulary, not vague answers. When you are shown a grazing diagram, examiners want you to read the layout: the direction the animals move, the equipment that controls them, and the named system. A strip grazing diagram shows animals given a fresh \'strip\' of grass each day, held by a movable (electric) fence that is shifted forward. Knowing the system name AND one real production advantage is where the marks are.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Name the fence AND the system, then give a production-based advantage',
      text: 'Students lose the easy marks by labelling the controlling barrier as just \'fence\' or \'wire\'. In strip grazing the marking scheme wants \'movable (electric) fence\' — the word movable/electric is what earns the label mark, because it is what defines the system. Then name the system exactly: \'strip grazing\'. For the advantage, do not write \'cows eat grass\'; give a managed-grassland benefit the scheme accepts, e.g. less grass wastage, leafier/more digestible grass, less poaching or soil compaction, or grass gets a chance to recover.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'The diagram shows a type of grazing system: a herd confined to a narrow band of fresh grass by a fence (labelled A) that is moved forward across the field each day, giving the animals a new band to graze. (a) Indicate the direction of grazing shown in the diagram. (b) Label A in the diagram. (c) Identify the type of grazing system shown. (d) Outline one advantage of this grazing system.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) The animals graze across the field in the direction the fence is advanced, working through one fresh strip at a time. (b) A is a movable (electric) fence — it is shifted forward each day to allocate a fresh strip of grass. (c) Strip grazing. (d) One advantage: it reduces grass wastage / gives grazed ground a chance to recover. (Other accepted advantages: leafier, more digestible grass; fewer weeds; less poaching or soil compaction; increased animal production.)',
      needed: ['(a) Direction of grazing read from the diagram (animals advance with the fence, one strip at a time)', '(b) (Movable) / (Electric) fence', '(c) Strip grazing', '(d) One advantage: less grass wastage OR grass gets a chance to recover OR more digestible/leafy grass OR fewer weeds OR less poaching/soil compaction OR increased animal production'],
    },
    source: 'Crops: Management — SEC LC Agricultural Science 2024 Ordinary Level, Q1 (a)-(d) + marking scheme (Q1: 3+3+2+2 = 10 marks; (b) \'(Moveable)(Electric) Fence\', (c) \'Strip grazing\')',
    marksWeight: 10,
