    id: 'es-preterito-vs-imperfecto-hl',
    subjectId: 'spanish', topicId: 'spanish-1-3',
    subjectLabel: 'Spanish', topicLabel: 'Pretérito vs imperfecto',
    focus: 'Pretérito = the completed event; imperfecto = the background/habit', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'When you write about the past (the diary, the dialogue, your opinion), Spanish forces a choice between two past tenses and the examiner reads it. The PRETÉRITO INDEFINIDO is a single completed event with a clear end: \'fui a Madrid\' (I went to Madrid), \'murió mi padre\' (my father died), \'ganó el Mundial\' (she won the World Cup). The IMPERFECTO is the background, the description, the habit or the ongoing scene: \'el estadio estaba lleno\' (the stadium was full), \'yo iba todos los veranos\' (I used to go every summer), \'hacía calor\' (it was hot). The classic combination is one inside the other: \'comía cuando llegó\' (I was eating when he arrived) — imperfecto for the scene, pretérito for the event that cuts in.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Event vs scene, not just \'past\'',
      text: 'Ask of each past verb: completed event with an endpoint (pretérito) or background / description / repeated habit (imperfecto)? Use the regular endings cleanly — pretérito: -é/-aste/-ó (hablé, habló) and -í/-iste/-ió (comí, comió); imperfecto: -aba/-aba (hablaba) and -ía/-ía (comía). So \'describe a day trip last month\' is pretérito (\'fuimos, vimos, comimos\' — we went, we saw, we ate), but \'the experience was amazing\' as ongoing background is imperfecto (\'era increíble\'). Mixing them up costs marks because the diary scheme only gives the top band per point when \'Verbs correct\', and \'Verbs must be correct for full marks\'.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Choose the right past tense for each gap and give the form. Context: a diary entry about a day trip. \'Ayer ____ (ir, nosotros) a la playa. ____ (Hacer) mucho sol y la gente ____ (estar) muy contenta. De repente ____ (empezar) a llover y ____ (volver, nosotros) a casa.\'',
      modelAnswer: 'Ayer FUIMOS a la playa (pretérito — completed event with \'ayer\'). HACÍA mucho sol (imperfecto — background weather/scene). La gente ESTABA muy contenta (imperfecto — ongoing description; \'la gente\' is singular, so 3rd-person estaba). De repente EMPEZÓ a llover (pretérito — single event that breaks the scene) y VOLVIMOS a casa (pretérito — completed action). Rule: the ongoing scene (sun, mood) is imperfecto; the things that happen and finish (went, started, returned) are pretérito.',
      needed: ['fuimos / volvimos / empezó in the pretérito (completed events)', 'hacía / estaba in the imperfecto (background description)', 'a one-line reason linking each choice to event-vs-scene'],
    },
    source: 'Topic Writing About Your Experience — SEC LC Spanish 2024 HL, Section C Q2(a) Diary Entry (\'Describe a day trip you took with your brother last month\' + \'Say that the experience was amazing. Explain why.\'). Marking scheme: Diary Entry = 20 marks total (four points communicated, max 5 marks each, 4 x 5m = 20). Per-point bands: 5 marks \'Communicative intention fulfilled. Verbs correct.\'; 4 marks \'Verbs generally correct.\'; Section C guideline \'Verbs must be correct for full marks.\'',
    marksWeight: 20,
