    id: 'es-ser-vs-estar-hl',
    subjectId: 'spanish', topicId: 'spanish-1-3',
    subjectLabel: 'Spanish', topicLabel: 'Ser vs estar',
    focus: 'Ser = identity/origin/inherent trait; estar = location and temporary state', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Both ser and estar mean \'to be\', but they are not interchangeable and examiners notice the wrong one. Use SER for identity, origin, nationality, profession, what something inherently IS, and the time: \'soy de Irlanda\' (I am from Ireland), \'es profesora\' (she is a teacher), \'la experiencia fue increíble\' (the experience was amazing). Use ESTAR for location and for temporary states/moods/conditions: \'estoy en Madrid\' (I am in Madrid), \'estoy muy contento\' (I am very happy), \'el estadio estaba lleno\' (the stadium was full), \'la casa está cerca\' (the house is nearby). The contrast can flip meaning: \'es aburrido\' = he is boring (trait) vs \'está aburrido\' = he is bored (state).',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Location & mood = ESTAR; identity & origin = SER',
      text: 'Before writing \'es\' or \'está\', label the idea: WHERE something is or HOW it temporarily feels → ESTAR (\'estoy contento\', \'está en Granada\', \'estamos cansados\'). WHAT/WHO it permanently is, where it\'s from, or a defining trait → SER (\'soy estudiante\', \'es de Sevilla\', \'la comida es deliciosa\'). A frequent recovery win: feelings and location are ESTAR, so \'I am happy\' is \'estoy contento/a\', never \'soy contento\'. Get ser/estar agreement right too: \'estoy cansada\' if you are female (the adjective agrees).',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Choose SER or ESTAR (correct form) for each gap, in a postcard from Granada. \'Hola, ____ en Granada con mi amiga, que ____ de Sevilla. La ciudad ____ preciosa y nosotras ____ muy contentas. El hotel ____ cerca del centro.\' (Glosses: I am in Granada / who is from Seville / the city is gorgeous / we are very happy / the hotel is near the centre.)',
      modelAnswer: 'ESTOY en Granada (estar — location). Mi amiga ES de Sevilla (ser — origin). La ciudad ES preciosa (ser — inherent quality/identity of the city). Nosotras ESTAMOS muy contentas (estar — temporary mood; note the -as agreement, two females). El hotel ESTÁ cerca del centro (estar — location). Rule: in/near/where = estar; from/what-it-is = ser; a passing feeling like \'contentas\' = estar.',
      needed: ['estoy / está / estamos for location and mood (estar)', 'es (de Sevilla) and es (preciosa) for origin and inherent quality (ser)', 'the contentas agreement plus a reason for each ser/estar choice'],
    },
    source: 'Topic Writing About Your Experience — SEC LC Spanish 2025 HL, Section A Q1(b) Journalistic Text (\'estar presente en el momento es más importante que ser puntual\') + Section C dialogue (\'estoy muy contento\') + marking scheme (Language band: \'Few grammatical/spelling mistakes\')',
    marksWeight: 25,
