    gist: 'Ordinary Section A reading texts are often about present-day Spanish-speaking culture (e.g. La Habana, Cuba — its nightlife, museums, classic cars, beaches and food like \'Moros y Cristianos\'). The instruction line tells you what LANGUAGE to answer in: the 2024 La Habana text says \'Lee este artículo... y contesta las preguntas en ESPAÑOL\', so every answer must be in Spanish. Get the language wrong and the scheme penalises you (50% of marks if the whole question is in the wrong language; 0m on any sub-part answered in the wrong language). The recovery move: read that instruction, then mirror it. At OL transcription is rewarded — \'Language manipulation is not tested and transcription (relevant and within reasonable limits) is acceptable\' — so lifting the exact relevant words from the text earns full marks.',
