    subjectLabel: 'Construction Studies', topicLabel: 'Superstructure',
    focus: 'Cavity wall with larch rainscreen: battens, counter-battens & full-fill cavity', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'The 2024 Q1 external wall was a 450 mm full-fill insulated cavity block wall finished with a 20 mm vertical larch cladding RAINSCREEN. A rainscreen is not a solid render finish: it is a ventilated/drained outer skin. The award points list, from outside in, the larch cladding rainscreen, battens / counter-battens (which create the drained-and-ventilated air gap behind the cladding), the cavity wall blockwork with low-conductivity wall ties, full-fill cavity insulation, a cavity closer and the wall plate. Each named-and-sized layer earns marks.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Battens AND counter-battens create the drained, ventilated cavity behind the cladding',
      text: 'Behind vertical larch cladding you must draw BOTH counter-battens (fixed back through the breather membrane to the blockwork) and battens, so the cladding stands off the wall on a ventilated and drained air gap. Any wind-driven rain that passes the boards runs down the back of the cladding and drains/evaporates, instead of soaking the insulated blockwork. Use LOW-CONDUCTIVITY wall ties across the full-fill cavity so they don\'t form cold bridges. Drawing the cladding flat against the blockwork with no air gap loses the rainscreen marks.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'An external cavity wall is finished with a 20 mm vertical larch cladding rainscreen over a 450 mm full-fill insulated cavity block wall. List the wall layers from the OUTSIDE (the larch) inward, and explain why battens/counter-battens are used behind the cladding rather than fixing the larch directly to the blockwork.',
      modelAnswer: 'Outside-in: 20 mm vertical larch cladding rainscreen, fixed to battens which are fixed to counter-battens, over a breather membrane on the outer concrete block leaf of the cavity wall; then the full-fill cavity insulation with low-conductivity wall ties tying the two leaves, the inner concrete block leaf, then internal plaster/skim. Battens and counter-battens hold the cladding off the wall to form a continuous ventilated and drained air gap: wind-driven rain that gets past the open-jointed larch runs down the back of the boards and drains away or evaporates in the ventilating air, so it never reaches the insulated blockwork. Low-conductivity wall ties are used so the ties bridging the full-fill cavity do not act as cold bridges.',
      needed: ['20 mm vertical larch cladding rainscreen as the outer layer', 'Battens / counter-battens forming a ventilated, drained air gap behind the cladding', 'Full-fill cavity insulation with low-conductivity wall ties', 'Cavity closer and wall plate / inner block leaf with internal skim'],
    },
    source: 'Topic Superstructure — SEC LC Construction Studies 2024 HL, Q1 + marking scheme (external wall & eaves award points: larch cladding rain screen, battens / counter battens, cavity wall blockwork & low-conductivity wall ties, full-fill insulation, cavity closer, wallplates, skim; external wall block = 4 x 4 marks; full Q1 = 60).',
    marksWeight: 16,
