      modelAnswer: '1) HEAVINESS (mass): heavyweight, high-density components vibrate less and transmit less sound — sound insulation rises about 5 dB each time the mass is doubled. Measure: 215 mm concrete block \'on the flat\', a precast concrete first floor instead of timber joists, or thicker (e.g. 12 mm) glass panes. 2) COMPLETENESS: insulation depends on airtightness and uniformity — any crack or hole has a major effect. Measure: airtight tape around door/window frames, full mortar joints with a complete plaster coat, staggered plasterboard joints, and sealed service sockets. 3) FLEXIBILITY: flexible materials absorb and dissipate sound energy. Measure: acoustic panels of fibreglass/mineral wool, acoustic sealant around openings, or a resilient floor underlay. 4) ISOLATION (discontinuous construction): breaking the connection between leaves so sound is converted/lost at each junction. Measure: a cavity between wall leaves, the argon-filled airspace in double/triple glazing, or a floating floor / \'room-within-a-room\' on decoupling fixings.',
