    id: 'art-sketchbooks-development-work-reflection-hl',
    subjectId: 'art', topicId: 'art-2-3',
    subjectLabel: 'Art', topicLabel: '3.4 Critical and Personal Reflection',
    focus: 'Value of sketchbooks/development work and viewing them digitally', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'The 2025 paper used the National Gallery of Ireland\'s digital archive \'Source\' to ask why an artist\'s sketchbooks and development work are worth studying alongside the finished piece, and the pros and cons of viewing a sketchbook on a screen. This is Critical and Personal Reflection: you reflect on PROCESS and on how we access art. Strong answers explain that sketchbooks reveal the artist\'s thinking, experiments, false starts and influences, and how an idea evolved into the final work. For the digital part, weigh real trade-offs: access from anywhere, zoom and preservation of fragile originals, against losing the true scale, texture, colour and physical experience of the object.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Reflect on process, then weigh the digital trade-off both ways',
      text: 'Don\'t just say \'sketchbooks are interesting\'. Explain what they show: the artist\'s research, trial compositions, colour tests, annotations and influences, so you see HOW the final work was reached and learn the working method. For the digital question, the scheme asks for advantages AND/OR disadvantages with explanation: name a genuine plus (free, remote, zoomable access; protects fragile originals; reaches a worldwide audience) and a genuine minus (you lose true scale, texture and colour; no physical presence; screen quality varies). Balancing both sides is what lifts you into the High band.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Q5(a): give two reasons it is useful to study an artist\'s sketchbooks and development work as well as the finished piece. Q5(b): give one advantage and one disadvantage of viewing a sketchbook on a digital platform.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) (1) Sketchbooks reveal the artist\'s thinking and process: their research, trial compositions, colour studies, annotations and the influences they drew on, so you understand HOW the final work developed rather than just seeing the end result. (2) They show experimentation and even abandoned ideas, which teaches the working method and helps a student artist learn how problems were solved and decisions were made on the way to the finished piece. (b) Advantage: a digital platform such as the National Gallery\'s \'Source\' gives free, remote access from anywhere, lets you zoom in on detail, and protects fragile original pages from handling while reaching a worldwide audience. Disadvantage: you lose the true experience of the object, its real scale, paper texture, exact colour and physical presence, and the quality depends on the screen and the digitisation, so it is not the same as viewing the original.',
      needed: ['Sketchbooks reveal the artist\'s thinking/process: research, trial compositions, colour studies, annotations, influences', 'They show experimentation/abandoned ideas, teaching the working method and how the final work evolved', 'Digital advantage: free remote zoomable access, protects fragile originals, reaches a worldwide audience', 'Digital disadvantage: loses true scale/texture/colour and physical presence; quality depends on screen/digitisation'],
    },
    source: 'Topic 3.4 Critical and Personal Reflection — SEC LC Art (Visual Studies) 2025 Higher Level, Section A, Q5 + marking scheme (\'why it might be useful to look at an artist\'s sketchbooks and development work\'; \'advantages and/or disadvantages of viewing a sketchbook on a digital platform\')',
    marksWeight: 10,
