    id: 'art-how-artists-earn-living-support-ol',
    subjectId: 'art', topicId: 'art-2-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Art', topicLabel: '3.3 Impact and Value',
    focus: 'How visual artists earn a living and who supports them', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'This Ordinary Level question is about the ECONOMIC value of art: how a visual artist actually makes money, and which organisation can support a professional artist\'s career. It is worth knowing the real ways artists earn (selling original works through a gallery or directly, commissions, prints and editions, teaching or running workshops, illustration or design work, grants and bursaries) and at least one named support body. In Ireland the Arts Council (An Chomhairle Ealaíon) funds artists through grants and bursaries; Visual Artists Ireland (VAI) is the representative organisation that gives advice, professional development and resources to visual artists.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'List two different income streams, then name a real organisation and say what it does',
      text: 'Part (a) needs TWO genuinely different ways to earn, each explained, not two versions of \'selling paintings\'. Pair, for example, selling original work through a gallery with running paid workshops/teaching, or commissions with prints/editions. Part (b) wants a NAMED organisation plus how it helps: name the Arts Council (grants and bursaries) or Visual Artists Ireland (advice, professional development, advocacy and resources). Naming a real body and explaining its support is what secures part (b).',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Q2(a): list and explain two different ways a visual artist earns a living from their practice. Q2(b): name one organisation that supports professional visual artists and explain how it helps.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) (1) Selling original artworks: the artist sells paintings, prints or sculptures through a gallery, an exhibition or directly to buyers, earning a fee or a share of the sale price. (2) Teaching and workshops, or commissioned/applied work: the artist earns a steadier income by teaching classes and running paid workshops, or by taking commissions and design/illustration jobs where a client pays them to make a specific piece. These are different streams because one depends on selling finished art while the other is paid for time, skills or a commission. (b) The Arts Council (An Chomhairle Ealaíon) supports professional visual artists by awarding grants, bursaries and project funding that pay for materials, studio time and the development of new work; (alternatively Visual Artists Ireland supports artists with professional advice, training, advocacy and resources).',
      needed: ['Selling original works (paintings/prints/sculpture) through a gallery, exhibition or directly to buyers', 'A second, different income stream such as teaching/paid workshops or commissions/design/illustration work', 'Names a real support organisation (e.g. the Arts Council / An Chomhairle Ealaíon, or Visual Artists Ireland)', 'Explains how it supports artists (grants/bursaries/funding, or advice/professional development/advocacy)'],
    },
    source: 'Topic 3.3 Impact and Value — SEC LC Art (Visual Studies) 2024 Ordinary Level, Section A, Q2 + marking scheme (\'two different ways that visual artists earn a living from their art practice\'; \'how a named organisation could support a professional visual artist\')',
