    id: 'art-galleries-tours-workshops-inform-artist-ol',
    subjectId: 'art', topicId: 'art-2-3',
    subjectLabel: 'Art', topicLabel: '3.4 Critical and Personal Reflection',
    focus: 'Why galleries run tours/workshops and how visiting informs an artist', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'This Ordinary Level question asks you to reflect on the VALUE of galleries and museums to the public and to artists. Part (a): why do public galleries and museums provide tours and workshops? Part (b): how might visiting a gallery or museum inform an artist\'s own work? Good answers connect the activity to a purpose: tours and workshops make art easier to understand, attract and educate new visitors (including families and schools), and build a community around the gallery. Visiting informs an artist by letting them study real works up close, learn techniques and ideas, find inspiration and influences, and reflect on how their own work compares.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Give a reason WHY for each, not just a description',
      text: 'The scheme repeats \'Give reasons for your answer\' for both parts, so every point needs a because. For (a): tours and workshops help non-experts understand the art (education), draw in wider and younger audiences, and make people feel welcome so they return. For (b): seeing originals shows the real scale, texture and colour you can\'t get from a screen or book, lets the artist study techniques and subjects, sparks ideas and influences, and lets them reflect critically on their own practice. Two reasoned points per part reach the High band.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Q4(a): give two reasons public galleries and museums provide tours and workshops. Q4(b): give two ways that visiting a gallery or museum might inform an artist\'s own work.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) (1) Tours and workshops educate visitors: a guide or facilitator explains the works so people who are not art experts can understand and appreciate what they are seeing, which makes the collection more meaningful. (2) They attract and widen the audience, especially families, schools and first-time visitors, and create an engaging, welcoming experience that encourages people to return and builds a community around the gallery. (b) (1) Seeing original works up close lets an artist study their true scale, texture, brushwork and colour, and learn techniques and approaches they cannot fully grasp from a book or screen. (2) Visiting exposes the artist to new ideas, subjects and influences that inspire their own work, and lets them reflect critically on how their practice compares, helping them develop and improve.',
      needed: ['Tours/workshops educate visitors and help non-experts understand and appreciate the art', 'Tours/workshops attract and widen the audience (families, schools, first-time visitors) and build engagement/community', 'Seeing originals up close lets an artist study real scale, texture, technique and colour', 'Visiting provides new ideas, subjects and influences and prompts reflection on the artist\'s own practice'],
    },
    source: 'Topic 3.4 Critical and Personal Reflection — SEC LC Art (Visual Studies) 2023 Ordinary Level, Section A, Q4 + marking scheme (\'Why you think public galleries and museums provide activities such as tours and workshops\'; \'How visiting an art gallery or museum might inform an artist\'s work\')',
    marksWeight: 10,
