    gist: 'The biggest word-order rule in German: in a main clause the conjugated verb is SECOND, but after a SUBORDINATING conjunction (weil \'because\', dass \'that\', obwohl \'although\', bevor \'before\', nachdem \'after\', wenn \'if/when\') the verb is kicked to the very END of its clause. \'Sie sagt, dass sie Jo suchen WIRD\' (she says that she will look for Jo). Applied Grammar Q2 tests this by giving you a text with conjunction gaps to fill; the verb position tells you which conjunction fits. Beware coordinating \'aber/und/oder\' — those DON\'T send the verb to the end.',
