    id: 'am-restricted-growth-learning-curve-hl',
    subjectId: 'applied-mathematics', topicId: 'applied-mathematics-3-3',
    subjectLabel: 'Applied Mathematics', topicLabel: 'Modelling Continuous Change with Differential Equations',
    focus: 'Restricted growth dN/dt = k(2000 − N): mind the minus when integrating', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Restricted (limited) growth models — learning curves, cooling, saturation — have the form dN/dt = k(limit − N): the rate slows as N approaches the limit. The exam asks you to separate the variables, solve for N, then use a data point to pin down k. The model should level off at the limit (here 2000 words); if your solution blows up instead, you have dropped a minus sign.',
    oneMove: {
      label: '∫dN/(2000 − N) = −ln(2000 − N), not +ln',
      text: 'Because d/dN(2000 − N) = −1, integrating dN/(2000 − N) gives −ln(2000 − N) + c. Losing that minus flips saturation into runaway growth and wrecks both the expression for N and the value of k. Then fit the constant from N(0) = 250 (which produces the 1750) and exponentiate both sides carefully.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'A student wishes to be able to spell 2000 difficult words. The rate of learning is modelled by dN/dt = k(2000 − N), where N(t) is the number of these words the student can spell after t hours and k is a positive constant. At the start the student can already spell 250 of the words, i.e. N(0) = 250. (a) Solve the differential equation to find an expression for N in terms of k and t. (b) After 6 hours the student can spell 1500 of the words. Calculate k.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) Separate: ∫dN/(2000 − N) = ∫k dt, so −ln(2000 − N) = kt + c. At t = 0, N = 250: −ln(1750) = c. So ln(1750/(2000 − N)) = kt, giving 2000 − N = 1750e^(−kt), i.e. N = 2000 − 1750e^(−kt). (b) N = 1500 at t = 6: 1500 = 2000 − 1750e^(−6k), so 1750e^(−6k) = 500, e^(6k) = 3.5, k = ln(3.5)/6 ≈ 0.209 hour⁻¹.',
      needed: ['Variables separated: ∫dN/(2000 − N) = ∫k dt, with the minus sign on ln(2000 − N)', 'Constant fixed from N(0) = 250, giving N = 2000 − 1750e^(−kt)', 'k = ln(3.5)/6 ≈ 0.209 hour⁻¹ (with the unit)'],
    },
    source: 'Topic: restricted growth / learning curve differential equation — SEC LC Applied Mathematics 2023 HL, Q7(b)(i)-(ii) + marking scheme (N = 2000 − 1750e^(−kt); k = ln(3.5)/6 ≈ 0.209 hour⁻¹)',
    marksWeight: 25,
