    id: 'am-vdvds-separable-substitution-hl',
    subjectId: 'applied-mathematics', topicId: 'applied-mathematics-3-4',
    subjectLabel: 'Applied Mathematics', topicLabel: 'Solving Differential Equations (Separable; Reducible Second-Order)',
    focus: 'v dv/ds equations: separate every v to the dv side before integrating', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'When acceleration depends on velocity or position, the second-order equation reduces to first order using a = v dv/ds. The exam then expects a clean separation: every v (with dv) on one side, every s (with ds) on the other, integrate both sides, and fix the constant from the initial conditions before solving for v. The 2024 HL question packaged all of this with an exponential that needs a substitution.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Divide across so the s side is pure ds',
      text: 'Rewrite v(dv/ds) = e^(v²/4) as ∫v·e^(−v²/4) dv = ∫ds — divide BOTH sides by e^(v²/4) so no v survives on the s side. Then the substitution u = −v²/4 (du = −(v/2)dv) gives ∫v·e^(−v²/4) dv = −2e^(−v²/4). Fix c from v = 0 at s = 0 BEFORE rearranging for v; bolting the constant on at the end gives the wrong curve through the wrong point.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'The acceleration of a particle moving in a straight line is expressed in terms of its velocity v (m s⁻¹) and displacement s (m) by the differential equation v(dv/ds) = e^(v²/4). (a) Solve the differential equation to find an expression for v in terms of s, given that v = 0 when s = 0. (b) Calculate v when s = 0.3 m.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) Separate: ∫v·e^(−v²/4) dv = ∫ds. With u = −v²/4, du = −(v/2)dv, the left side is −2∫e^u du = −2e^(−v²/4). So −2e^(−v²/4) = s + c. At v = 0, s = 0: −2 = c. Then −2e^(−v²/4) = s − 2, so e^(−v²/4) = (2 − s)/2, giving −v²/4 = ln((2 − s)/2), i.e. v² = 4ln(2/(2 − s)) and v = 2√(ln(2/(2 − s))) m s⁻¹. (b) s = 0.3: v = 2√(ln(2/1.7)) = 2√(0.1625) = 0.81 m s⁻¹.',
      needed: ['Correct separation ∫v·e^(−v²/4) dv = ∫ds and the substitution result −2e^(−v²/4)', 'Constant from v = 0 at s = 0: c = −2, leading to v = 2√(ln(2/(2 − s)))', 'v = 0.81 m s⁻¹ when s = 0.3 m (value with unit)'],
    },
    source: 'Topic: separable differential equations with a = v dv/ds — SEC LC Applied Mathematics 2024 HL, Q6(b) + marking scheme (−2e^(−v²/4) = s + c with c = −2; v = 2√(ln(2/(2 − s))) m s⁻¹; v = 0.81 m s⁻¹ at s = 0.3)',
    marksWeight: 25,
