    id: 'am-ol-kruskal-mst-2025-q9a',
    subjectId: 'applied-mathematics', topicId: 'applied-mathematics-1-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Applied Mathematics', topicLabel: 'Minimum Spanning Trees (Kruskal and Prim)',
    focus: 'Using Kruskal\'s algorithm to build a minimum spanning tree from a distance table', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Kruskal builds the cheapest network that links every node. Sort ALL edges from shortest to longest, then go down the list adding each edge UNLESS it would close a loop with edges you already kept. Stop the instant you have n-1 edges (here 7 houses, so 6 pipes). The marks are lost two ways: starting from a node instead of from the sorted edge-list (that\'s Prim, not Kruskal), and either skipping the cycle-check or not stopping at n-1.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Sort every edge first, then reject any edge that re-links an already-linked group',
      text: 'Write out ALL the pipe lengths in ascending order before you pick anything (4, 5, 5, 12, 13, 14, 15, 15, 16, 16, 17, 17, 25, 26, 27). Take them in that order. Before keeping an edge, ask: are its two houses ALREADY joined through pipes I\'ve kept? If yes, it makes a loop, so reject it. Keep going until you have exactly n-1 = 6 pipes, then stop, even if cheaper-looking edges remain. The classic OL error is jumping to the smallest pipe touching house A and growing outward (that is Prim) or forgetting to stop at 6.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Seven houses A-G are linked by pipes; the table gives pipe lengths in m, where a dash means no direct pipe. Symmetric distances: AB=14, AC=15, AD=16, AE=26; BD=4, BE=15, BF=13; CD=5, CF=17, CG=27; DE=17, DF=16, DG=25; EG=5; FG=12. Using Kruskal\'s algorithm, find the minimum spanning tree, and calculate the total length of piping on it.',
      modelAnswer: 'List all edges ascending: BD=4, CD=5, EG=5, FG=12, BF=13, AB=14, AC=15, BE=15, AD=16, DF=16, CF=17, DE=17, DG=25, AE=26, CG=27. Apply Kruskal, rejecting any edge that closes a loop, stopping at n-1 = 6 edges (7 houses). Add BD=4 (total 4). Add CD=5 (total 9). Add EG=5 (total 14). Add FG=12 (total 26). Add BF=13 (total 39) — this joins the {B,C,D} group to the {E,F,G} group. Add AB=14 (total 53) — this is the first edge that connects house A, the last unlinked node. Now there are 6 edges joining all 7 houses, so stop. MST edges = {BD, CD, EG, FG, BF, AB}. Total length = 4 + 5 + 5 + 12 + 13 + 14 = 53 m.',
      needed: ['MST is exactly the six edges BD, CD, EG, FG, BF, AB (chosen in ascending order, each rejected if it would form a cycle, stopping at n-1 = 6 edges for 7 nodes)', 'Total piping length = 4 + 5 + 5 + 12 + 13 + 14 = 53 m (correct value with unit m)'],
    },
    source: 'Topic 1.2 Minimum Spanning Trees (Kruskal and Prim) — SEC LC Applied Mathematics 2025 Ordinary Level, Q9(a)(i)-(ii) + marking scheme (9ai: |BD|=4, |CD|=5, |EG|=5, |GF|=12, |BF|=13, |AB|=14; 9aii: 4+5+5+12+13+14=53)',
    marksWeight: 25,
