    id: 'am-ol-networks-terminology-adjacency-matrix-2023',
    subjectId: 'applied-mathematics', topicId: 'applied-mathematics-1-0',
    subjectLabel: 'Applied Mathematics', topicLabel: 'Networks and Their Associated Terminology',
    focus: 'Reading a network: counting vertices and edges, spotting loops and parallel edges, and writing the adjacency matrix', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'An adjacency matrix is just a tally table: entry (row X, column Y) = how many edges directly join vertex X to vertex Y. For an undirected graph it is symmetric, parallel edges show as a number bigger than 1, and a loop sits on the diagonal. Read the network carefully edge-by-edge and you get the matrix for free.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Tally every edge, including the sneaky ones',
      text: 'Go vertex by vertex and count the edges leaving each one. The two marks people drop are the parallel edges (A to E has TWO edges, so write 2, not 1) and the loop at D (it touches only D, so it lands on the diagonal D-D, not on any other cell). Then sanity-check that the matrix is symmetric about the diagonal.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'A graph has five vertices A, B, C, D, E joined by these edges: A-B (one edge), A-E (TWO parallel edges), B-C (one edge), C-D (one edge), D-E (one edge), and a single loop at D (an edge from D back to D). (i) Write the adjacency matrix for this graph, with rows and columns in the order A, B, C, D, E. (ii) State how many edges the graph has in total. (iii) Write down the degree of vertex B.',
      modelAnswer: '(i) Entry (X,Y) = number of edges joining X to Y. Working through each vertex and using a 1 on the diagonal for the loop at D (as in the SEC scheme):\n\n      A  B  C  D  E\n  A [ 0  1  0  0  2 ]\n  B [ 1  0  1  0  0 ]\n  C [ 0  1  0  1  0 ]\n  D [ 0  0  1  1  1 ]\n  E [ 2  0  0  1  0 ]\n\nA-E = 2 (parallel edges), the loop puts a 1 in cell (D,D), and the matrix is symmetric about the diagonal as required for an undirected graph.\n\n(ii) Count the distinct edges: A-B, A-E, A-E, B-C, C-D, D-E, and the loop at D = 7 edges in total.\n\n(iii) Degree of B = number of edge-ends meeting B = the edges A-B and B-C = degree 2 (matching row B of the matrix: 1+0+1+0+0 = 2).',
      needed: ['Correct adjacency matrix with A-E = 2 (parallel edges) and the loop shown as a 1 on the D-D diagonal; matrix symmetric', 'Total edges = 7 (counting both parallel A-E edges and the loop) and degree of B = 2'],
    },
    source: 'Topic 1.0 Networks and Their Associated Terminology — SEC LC Applied Mathematics 2023 Ordinary Level, Q1(a)(i) + marking scheme (1(a)(i), 10 marks [0/4/7])',
