    subjectId: 'politics-and-society', topicId: 'politics-and-society-1-8',
    subjectLabel: 'Politics & Society', topicLabel: 'Resolving Conflicts',
    focus: 'A strategy for conflict in small groups', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Working as an active citizen means handling disagreement inside a group without it derailing the action. This Short Answer item asks for ONE strategy for dealing with conflict in small groups, and the scheme gives a \'very good explanation\' the top band (9-10M of 10). A one-word answer like \'compromise\' with no explanation sits in the weak band - you must name the strategy and explain how it actually defuses the conflict.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Name one concrete strategy and explain how it resolves the conflict',
      text: 'Pick a single, specific strategy and explain its mechanism. Strong choices: active listening (each person is heard so misunderstandings are cleared up), seeking compromise/finding common ground, using a neutral third party to mediate, or agreeing ground rules so the group focuses on the issue not the person. The marks come from the explanation - say what the strategy is AND how it lowers the conflict, rather than just naming it.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Short Answer item (10 marks): \'Give one example of a strategy that could be used when dealing with conflict in small groups.\' Name one strategy and explain how it helps resolve the conflict.',
      modelAnswer: 'Strategy: Active listening. When a conflict arises in a small group, the members take turns to set out their view while everyone else listens without interrupting, and then each person reflects back what they heard. This works because a lot of group conflict comes from misunderstanding or from people feeling ignored. By making sure each person is genuinely heard, active listening lowers defensiveness, separates the real issue from the personality clash, and lets the group move toward a compromise or shared solution that everyone can accept. (Other valid strategies include using a neutral mediator, agreeing ground rules, or seeking common ground/compromise.)',
      needed: ['Names one specific conflict-resolution strategy (e.g. active listening, compromise/common ground, mediation by a neutral third party, agreed ground rules)', 'Explains HOW the strategy reduces or resolves the conflict, not just names it', 'Keeps it appropriate to a small group working together (e.g. an action team or class group)'],
    },
    source: 'Topic Resolving Conflicts - SEC LC Politics & Society 2021 Ordinary Level, Section A, Q1(k) + marking scheme (example of a strategy for dealing with conflict in small groups; 10 marks; very good explanation 9-10M, good 7-8M, fair 4-6M, weak 0-3M)',
    marksWeight: 10,
