    gist: 'A valid conclusion is one the data on the graph or in the table actually shows. The exam often lists several conclusions and asks you to tick the ones supported by the data and cross the ones that are not. A statement can only be supported if you can read the evidence for it from the graph: claims about things the experiment never measured (e.g. which food is \'healthier\') can never be supported, and a claim that the graph doesn\'t actually back (e.g. one line rising faster than another when both rise at a similar rate) gets a cross too. Don\'t tick something just because it sounds true.',
