    id: 'jcsci-accuracy-precision-true-value',
    subjectId: 'jc-science', topicId: 'jc-science-0-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Science (Junior Cycle)', topicLabel: 'Designing fair tests: variables, controls, reliability, accuracy, precision & safety',
    focus: 'Accuracy vs precision: accuracy = closeness to the TRUE value; precision = closeness of repeated readings to each other. They are independent.', level: 'common',
    gist: 'Accuracy and precision are NOT the same thing, and a set of results can have one without the other. Accuracy means how close a measurement is to the TRUE (correct) value. Precision means how close your repeated measurements are to EACH OTHER, regardless of whether they are right. So readings that are tightly clustered but all sitting away from the true value are precise but not accurate, while readings landing right on the true value but scattered are accurate-on-average but not precise. The ideal result is both: clustered tightly AND on the true value.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'The trap to avoid',
      text: 'Don\'t assume \'all the same\' means \'correct\'. To judge PRECISION, only look at how close the repeats are to each other (a tight cluster = precise). To judge ACCURACY, compare them to the given TRUE value. Identical readings that are all off the true value are precise but NOT accurate — that is the classic mark-loser.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Three students measured the total volume of carbon dioxide produced when excess marble chips reacted with 100 cm3 of dilute hydrochloric acid. Each repeated the run three times. The correct (true) total volume is 240 cm3. Their results were: Student 1 = 220, 210, 230 cm3; Student 2 = 240, 240, 240 cm3; Student 3 = 220, 220, 220 cm3. (a) Which student\'s data were PRECISE but NOT accurate? (b) Which student\'s data were NEITHER accurate nor precise? Justify each choice by referring to the true value of 240 cm3.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) Student 3 — precise but not accurate. (b) Student 1 — neither accurate nor precise. (Student 2\'s data are both accurate and precise.)',
      needed: ['(a) Student 3: the three readings are identical (220, 220, 220) so they are very close to each other = precise; but they are all 20 cm3 below the true value of 240 cm3, so they are not accurate.', '(b) Student 1: the readings (220, 210, 230) are spread out / not close to each other = not precise, and their average (~220) is below the true 240 cm3 = not accurate.', 'Correct distinction stated: precision = how close the repeats are to EACH OTHER; accuracy = how close they are to the TRUE value (240 cm3).'],
    },
