    id: 'jcsci-phases-eclipses-why',
    subjectId: 'jc-science', topicId: 'jc-science-4-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Science (Junior Cycle)', topicLabel: 'The Earth-Sun-Moon system: day & night, seasons, lunar phases & eclipses',
    focus: 'Why we see Moon phases (the half-lit Moon viewed from different orbital positions) versus why a solar eclipse needs the Moon lined up directly between Sun and Earth, making eclipses rare', level: 'common',
    gist: 'The Sun always lights up half the Moon, but as the Moon orbits the Earth we see that lit half from different angles, so the lit portion we can see grows (waxing) and shrinks (waning) through the phases. Phases are NOT caused by the Earth\'s shadow falling on the Moon. A solar eclipse is different: it happens only when the Moon moves directly between the Sun and the Earth so the Moon blocks the Sun\'s light from reaching us. This needs the three bodies in a near-perfect line, which is why eclipses are rare even though the Moon orbits every month.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Phases vs eclipse — don\'t mix them up',
      text: 'A phase is just which part of the Moon\'s permanently half-lit surface we can see from a given orbital position. An eclipse only happens when Sun, Moon and Earth line up so one body blocks light: SOLAR eclipse = Moon between Sun and Earth (Moon blocks the Sun, happens at New Moon, phase showing ~0% lit); LUNAR eclipse = Earth between Sun and Moon (Earth\'s shadow falls on a Full Moon, ~100% lit). State the cause as light being blocked by a body in the line, not as a normal phase.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'As the Moon orbits the Earth, the percentage of its surface visible from Ireland changes through eight phases. In one phase about 0% of the Moon is visible, in another about 100% is visible. (a) During which of these two situations could a TOTAL SOLAR eclipse (an eclipse of the Sun) occur, and explain why a solar eclipse only happens when the Moon lies directly between the Sun and the Earth. (b) Why are total solar eclipses rare rather than happening every month?',
      modelAnswer: '(a) A total solar eclipse can occur at the phase where ~0% of the Moon is visible (the New Moon phase). At this point the Moon is between the Sun and the Earth, so its sunlit half faces away from us and it appears dark. A solar eclipse happens because the Moon blocks the Sun\'s light from reaching the Earth, which can only occur when the Moon is directly in line between the Sun and the Earth, casting its shadow onto the Earth. (b) The Moon\'s orbit is slightly tilted, so at most New Moons the Moon passes a little above or below the Sun-Earth line and no shadow lands on Earth. A total solar eclipse needs an almost exact alignment of Sun, Moon and Earth, which happens only occasionally, so eclipses are rare even though there is a New Moon every month.',
      needed: ['Identifies the ~0% visible / New Moon phase as when a solar eclipse can occur', 'States the Moon blocks (the Sun\'s) light from reaching the Earth / casts its shadow on Earth, requiring the Moon directly between Sun and Earth', 'Explains rarity: the Moon usually passes above or below the Sun-Earth line, so an almost exact alignment is needed and only happens occasionally'],
    },
