    id: 'jcbus-international-trade-balance-of-trade',
    subjectId: 'jc-business-studies', topicId: 'jc-business-studies-2-6',
    subjectLabel: 'Business Studies (Junior Cycle)', topicLabel: 'International trade & globalisation',
    focus: 'Imports vs exports and the balance of trade (visible exports − visible imports)', level: 'common',
    gist: 'Exports = goods/services we SELL abroad (money comes IN). Imports = goods/services we BUY from abroad (money goes OUT). \'Visible\' means physical goods you can see (beef, cars, oil); \'invisible\' means services (tourism, insurance, banking). The Balance of Trade = visible exports − visible imports. If exports are bigger you get a SURPLUS (good); if imports are bigger you get a DEFICIT. The whole topic turns on getting the DIRECTION right — who is selling to whom decides import vs export.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Follow the money: who buys, who sells',
      text: 'Before ticking a box or doing the sum, ask \'is money coming INTO Ireland or going OUT?\' Money IN = export (we sold it). Money OUT = import (we bought it). \'Irish beef sold in Britain\' → Ireland sells, money in → visible EXPORT. \'German cars sold in Ireland\' → Ireland buys, money out → visible IMPORT. Then for the Balance of Trade always write it the same way round: Visible Exports − Visible Imports. Exports first. A positive answer is a surplus, a negative answer is a deficit — never flip the order.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'Ireland trades with countries all over the world. (a) For EACH statement, say whether it is a Visible Import, an Invisible Import, or a Visible Export: (1) Irish beef sold in Britain; (2) Irish people holidaying in Spain; (3) German cars sold in Ireland. (b) In one year Ireland\'s visible exports were €210 billion and its visible imports were €140 billion. Calculate the Balance of Trade and state clearly whether it is a SURPLUS or a DEFICIT.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) (1) Irish beef sold in Britain = Visible Export (Ireland sells a physical good abroad, money comes in). (2) Irish people holidaying in Spain = Invisible Import (Ireland buys a foreign service — tourism — money goes out). (3) German cars sold in Ireland = Visible Import (Ireland buys a physical good from abroad, money goes out). (b) Balance of Trade = Visible Exports − Visible Imports = €210bn − €140bn = €70 billion. Because exports are greater than imports the answer is positive, so this is a TRADE SURPLUS of €70 billion.',
      needed: ['Direction is correct on all three: beef = Visible Export, Spain holiday = Invisible Import, German cars = Visible Import (money-in = export, money-out = import; service = invisible).', 'Balance of Trade computed as Exports − Imports in that order: €210bn − €140bn = €70bn (not €140 − €210).', 'States it is a SURPLUS, justified because exports (€210bn) exceed imports (€140bn).'],
    },
    source: 'Topic 2.6 International trade & globalisation — SEC JC Business Studies 2025 CL, Section B Q (b)(i) visible/invisible import-export classification table [9m] + marking scheme (accepted: Irish beef sold in Britain = Visible Export; Irish people holidaying in Spain = Invisible Import; German cars sold in Ireland = Visible Import). Balance of Trade definition (visible exports − visible imports) per the JC Business Studies specification.',
    marksWeight: 9,
